<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002858> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002858";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-06-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Asexual people’s legal rights to be informed, present, and heard within the criminal justice system after having been harmed or injured as the result of a crime, accident, or other event."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001492>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000702>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Asexual victims' rights"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002859> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002859";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-06-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Businesses focusing on asexual clientele and products related to asexual communities."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001741>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001741>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Asexual-focused businesses"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002860> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002860";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-06-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Businesses owned by asexual people."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001735>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001735>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Asexual-owned businesses"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002861> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002861";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with Jacobs syndrome develop with an extra Y chromosome. They might not have any obvious physical variations as a result, or they may be taller than usual or have other identifiable differences that do not relate to sex characteristics. Some people with Jacobs syndrome may also have a smaller-than-typical penis or scrotum, hypospadias, and/or undescended testicles."@en;
  skos:altLabel "47XYY"@en,
    "XYY syndrome"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Jacobs syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002862> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002862";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Kallmann syndrome can occur in people with XX or XY chromosomes and is a form of hypogonadism. People with Kallmann syndrome and XY chromosomes are often born with a smaller-than-typical penis and undescended testes. In adolescence, most people with Kallmann syndrome will either start puberty later than usual or not experience typical pubertal changes (such as developing facial hair and a deeper voice for people with XY chromosomes or developing breasts and starting to menstruate for people with XX chromosomes) unless they receive hormone therapy. Kallmann syndrome also affects someone’s sense of smell."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Kallmann syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002864> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002864";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with XY chromosomes and luteinizing hormone insensitivity develop the variation Leydig cell hypoplasiae. Because people with XX chromosomes do not typically produce Leydig cells to begin with, someone with XX chromosomes who has a genetic insensitivity to luteinizing hormone will not be affected in the same way. A person with XX chromosomes whose body does not respond to luteinizing hormone will not usually have any noticeable differences at birth, but their ovaries may not produce fertile follicles (eggs), and they may not menstruate."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Luteinizing hormone insensitivity"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002866> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002866";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-09-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with mild androgen insensitivity syndrome (MAIS) respond to testosterone at a somewhat reduced level compared to typical levels, which means that they will usually be born with a penis (which may or may not be smaller than typical) and will later develop secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty. Because their bodies convert a small amount of their naturally produced androgens into estrogen, they may have visible variations in features, such as the amount of facial hair, body hair, or muscle tone they develop. They may also experience some breast development during puberty."@en;
  skos:altLabel "MAIS"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000044>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Mild androgen insensitivity syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002867> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002867";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-09-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with mixed gonadal dysgenesis are born with a mosaic (usually 45X/46XY) chromosome pattern and may develop some gonadal streak tissue and testicular tissue. They may be born with a vulva or penis, or with visible genital variations. They usually have a partially or completely developed uterus, and they may have combinations of internal reproductive structures such as a fallopian tube and a vas deferens on opposite sides of the body. In adolescence, people with mixed gonadal dysgenesis may develop some secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty, depending on the amount of hormone-producing testicular tissue they have."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Partial gonadal dysgenesis"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000599>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Mixed gonadal dysgenesis"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002869> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002869";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-09-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), a subtype of CAH, does not usually become apparent until later in childhood, adolescence, or even young adulthood."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000301>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002870> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002870";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with penoscrotal transposition are born with a different genital configuration than typical, with their penis located below or in the middle of their scrotum instead of above it. Their scrotum might be bifid (split into two halves), and they may also have hypospadias and/or chordee."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Penoscrotal transposition"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002871> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002871";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome (PMDS) have XY chromosomes, male-typical reproductive organs and external genitalia, and some component(s) of a female-typical reproductive tract, such as a uterus, fallopian tubes, and/or upper vaginal canal. PMDS occurs when the Müllerian ducts—internal structures that ordinarily break down in an XY fetus—remain and develop as they would in an XX fetus. PMDS is ordinarily not diagnosed at birth, and individuals with this variation often have a male gender identity."@en;
  skos:altLabel "PMDS"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002872> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002872";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with this variation have XX chromosomes and are generally born with a vulva and vagina, a uterus, and ovaries. Later in life, their ovaries often produce higher-than-typical levels of androgens like testosterone. Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can also result in the development of characteristics such as facial and body hair, and it can interfere with the process of ovulation. This in turn can cause irregular periods or no periods, and people with PCOS may struggle to become pregnant. Some people with PCOS have small cysts (fluid-filled sacs) on their ovaries, but some do not. Signs of PCOS generally will not show up until a person starts menstruating, or later in life."@en;
  skos:altLabel "PCOD"@en,
    "PCOS"@en,
    "Polycystic ovarian disease"@en,
    "Polycystic ovarian syndrome"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Polycystic ovary syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002873> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002873";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "A term previously used in medical contexts to refer to different groups of intersex variations, usually divided into categories of “male pseudohermaphroditism” and “female pseudohermaphroditism” on the basis of the person’s chromosomes. People may still find these terms used in their medical records or in medical journal articles when researching their variation, but they are often considered highly stigmatizing in addition to being vague and inaccurate. Do not use this term to describe a person's variation unless they do so themselves."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Female pseudohermaphroditism"@en,
    "Male pseudohermaphroditism"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Pseudohermaphroditism"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002874> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002874";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-09-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People who have salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), a subtype of classical CAH, experience \"salt-wasting,\" a dangerous scenario a person's low levels of aldosterone cause their body to lose too much sodium. They may experience life-threatening adrenal crises, especially when their body is under stress."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002827>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002875> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002875";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "A term sometimes used in medical contexts to refer to different variations in which a person is born with XY chromosomes and a vulva and vagina or XX chromosomes and a penis."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Sex reversal syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002877> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002877";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-09-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Minimally developed gonadal tissue that is present in place of either testes or ovaries. Streak gonads are more common in certain intersex variations such as Swyer syndrome."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000600>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Streak gonads"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002878> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002878";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with Trisomy X are born with 47XXX chromosomes rather than the typical 46XX. If they receive the additional X chromosome from their parent's original sperm or egg cell, they will have 47XXX chromosomes in all their cells. If the additional chromosome appears during the embryo's development process, however, only some cells will have 47XXX chromosomes and the person will be born with a mosaic chromosome pattern. Most people with 47XXX chromosomes do not develop any variations in their other sex characteristics but some may have ovaries that stop producing hormones at an earlier-than-typical age and some may not menstruate."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Triple XXX syndrome"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Trisomy X"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002879> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002879";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-09-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with Type 1 Leydig cell hypoplasia (LCH) produce almost no testosterone and will usually be born with a vulva and vagina and undescended testes. They are usually assigned female at birth, often without their variation being recognized until adolescence, at which point they generally will not develop any secondary sex characteristics or experience other changes typically associated with puberty."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Type 1 LCH"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002863>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Type 1 Leydig cell hypoplasia"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002880> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002880";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-10-27"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with Type 1 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome typically have only the differences associated with MRKH syndrome."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Type 1 MRKH"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002865>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Type 1 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002881> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002881";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-09-14"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with Type 2 Leydig cell hypoplasia (LCH) have some response to luteinizing hormone and produce lower-than-typical amounts of testosterone, but more than people with Type 1 LCH. At birth, their testes may be descended or undescended, and they may have a smaller-than-typical penis, often with other noticeable differences such as hypospadias. In adolescence, they will often develop some characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty."@en;
  skos:altLabel "Type 2 LCH"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002863>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Type 2 Leydig cell hypoplasia"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002882> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002882";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2023-10-27"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with Type 2 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome have both the differences associated with MRKH syndrome and additional differences in other parts of their body, such as their fallopian tubes, kidneys, spine, or heart. A specific example of Type 2 MRKH known as Müllerian agenesis, Renal agenesis, Cervicothoracic Somite (MURCS) association, occurs when someone’s kidneys do not develop typically. They may have scoliosis or fused vertebrae in addition to the usual features of MRKH. "@en;
  skos:altLabel "Type 2 MRKH"@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002865>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Type 2 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002883> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002883";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with this variation may be born without a vaginal opening, a shallow “dimple” where an opening would typically be, or a vaginal opening that is fused or blocked by fibrous tissue. People with vaginal atresia often have a typical upper vaginal canal (along with a typical uterus, cervix, fallopian tubes, and ovaries) with differences in the lower portion of the vagina only."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Vaginal atresia"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002885> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002885";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-01-30"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "People with XXYY syndrome are born with an extra copy of both an X and Y chromosome, resulting in an 48XXYY chromosome pattern instead of the typical 46XY. People with XXYY chromosomes may have undescended testes and produce lower-than-typical amounts of testosterone, which can cause them to start puberty at a later age than typical and develop less pronounced secondary sex characteristics like muscle tone and facial hair. They may also experience breast growth. People with XXYY syndrome often have other physical features that are similar to those developed by people with Klinefelter syndrome (47XXY)."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "XXYY syndrome"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002887> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002887";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-05-24"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Operas created by gay directors, depicting gay men, or that are regarded as important within gay culture."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001345>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Gay operas"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002888> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002888";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-05-24"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Operas created by bisexual directors, depicting bisexual individuals, or that are regarded as important within bisexual culture."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001345>;
  skos:inScheme <https://homosaurus.org/v3>;
  skos:prefLabel "Bisexual operas"@en .

<https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002890> a skos:Concept;
  dc:identifier "homoit0002890";
  dc:issued "2023-06-28"^^xsd:date;
  dc:modified "2024-05-24"^^xsd:date;
  rdfs:comment "Operas created by asexual directors, depicting asexual individuals, or that are regarded as important within asexual culture."@en;
  skos:broader <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886>;
  skos:hasTopConcept <https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001345>;
